{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/indirect-discrimination",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "indirect discrimination"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "Discrimination that arises when a seemingly neutral provision, criterion, or practice disproportionately disadvantages persons with a protected characteristic, unless the measure is objectively justified by a legitimate aim and the means of achieving it are appropriate and necessary. For example: a workplace rule requiring full-time on-site presence may disproportionately exclude employees with disabilities or caregiving responsibilities if reasonable adjustments could achieve the same goal without exclusion."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "This concept, developed in U.S. equal protection and EU equality law, recognises that discrimination often operates structurally, through rules and decisions that perpetuate inequality even without explicit bias"
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "6.3.5"
  ],
